multiHilbertPolynomial -- Multi-graded Hilbert polynomial for a product of projective spaces
********************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        H = multiHilbertPolynomial M
        H = multiHilbertPolynomial (M, VariableName => "s")
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, multigraded over a polynomial
        ring
      * VariableName, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * VariableName => ..., default value "s"
  * Outputs:
      * H, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, in QQ[h]

Description
===========

Let M be a module over a polynomial ring P =
kk[x_{0,0}..x_{0,a_0}..x_{n-1,0}..x_{n-1,a_{n-1}}] graded with degree x_{i,j} =
e_i, the i-th unit vector. If M = P^{m} is free, then the Hilbert polynomial is
the product of the shifted binomial coefficients binomial(a_i+m_i+t,a_i). In
general, the routine computes a free resolution of the coker of the initial
matrix of a presentation matrix, and then makes an alternating sum of the
Hilbert polynomials of the free modules in the resolution. The polynomial
returned has variables s_i (the default) or name_i if VariableName => "name" is
given.

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : P = productOfProjectiveSpaces{1,2}|
|                                       |
|o1 = P                                 |
|                                       |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                    |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : M1 = P^1                          |
|                                       |
|      1                                |
|o2 = P                                 |
|                                       |
|o2 : P-module, free                    |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : multiHilbertPolynomial M1         |
|                                       |
|     1   2   3       1 2        3      |
|o3 = -s s  + -s s  + -s  + s  + -s  + 1|
|     2 0 1   2 0 1   2 1    0   2 1    |
|                                       |
|o3 : QQ[s ..s ]                        |
|         0   1                         |
+---------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Because of the computation of a free resolution, this might be slow on large
examples.

See also
========

  * *note correspondencePolynomial: correspondencePolynomial, -- computes the
    Hilbert polynomial of a correspondence scroll

Ways to use multiHilbertPolynomial:
===================================

  * "multiHilbertPolynomial(Module)"

